WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.893
Think of a kitchen. Flour goes in a jar, eggs in a box, and milk in a bottle. Each container suits one kind of thing. A computer is the same. Every value is stored with a data type, which says what kind of value it is and what can be done with it.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:41.359
There are five data types to know. An integer is a whole number, like nineteen. A real number has a decimal part, like four point five. A Boolean is only True or False. A char is one single character, like a capital A. A string is a run of characters, like the word Hello.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:01:04.262
In Python, it looks like this. Hours equals nineteen is an integer. Price equals three five six point two five is a real, which Python calls a float. Member equals True is a Boolean. Grade holds one character, so it is a char, and name holds a string. Text always sits inside quotation marks.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:26.093
Ask three questions in order. Is it True or False? Then it is a Boolean. Is it a number? A whole number is an integer, and any decimal point makes it real. Is it text? One character is a char. More than one is a string.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:39.585
If you must justify, point to the feature you used. For a real, say it has a decimal part, or that it is made by multiplying by nought point five. Saying it is a number is not enough.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:54.376
Here is a real question from Paper 2 in June 2024. Complete the table to show the data type for each item: forty-five point eight two, and True. It is worth two marks.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:02:03.989
The first item is forty-five point eight two. It is a number with a decimal point, so it is a real number. In Python this is a float.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:12.027
The second item is True, with no quotation marks. True is one of only two possible values, so it is a Boolean.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:29.626
You can check this in Python. The type function shows float for forty-five point eight two, and bool, short for Boolean, for True. So write real, then Boolean, for one mark each. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:38.096
A common mistake is calling True a string. Without quotation marks, True is a Boolean.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:44.575
Another mistake is giving a whole word as an example of a char. A char is one character only.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:59.795
Now you try. Give the best data type for four values: the letter Q in quotation marks, seventeen, three point nought, and seventeen inside quotation marks. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:08.258
The letter Q is one character, so it is a char. Seventeen is a whole number, so it is an integer.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:21.055
Three point nought has a decimal point, so it is real, even though its value is whole. Seventeen inside quotation marks is text, so it is a string. You cannot do sums with it.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:33.374
Remember the method: is it True or False, is it a number, or is it text? Then justify with the feature you spotted. Now try the practice questions for this type.
